In this lesson we'll look at how to actually work with surds, not just leave them sitting there on their own. The big idea is that surds follow rules that come straight from the index laws, so you can multiply, divide, and tidy them up just like normal numbers.

First up, multiplying and dividing. You can combine square roots by multiplying or dividing the bits inside. So the square root of a times the square root of b is just the square root of a times b. And the square root of a divided by the square root of b is the square root of a over b. Simple.

Next, simplifying. When you've got a surd, peek inside the root and see if there's a perfect square hiding there. If there is, pull it out in front. The goal is to leave nothing under the root except numbers that don't have a square factor, other than one.

Then, adding and subtracting. You can only combine surds when they're like terms, meaning the bit inside the root is exactly the same. So three root five plus two root five is five root five. But root two plus root three, you just leave it.

Finally, rationalising the denominator. You don't want a surd on the bottom of a fraction, so you multiply by a clever form of one that clears it, usually using a difference of squares trick.

Now let's see it in action.